<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 921–922</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Ibn Fadlan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad ibn Fadlan was an Arab member of the embassy sent by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Muqtadir to the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> in 921 to 922. His account describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, the Bulgars' conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and a Rus' ship burial on the Volga. It is one of the main written sources for the peoples of the 10th-century steppe.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Contemporary sources call Ahmad ibn Fadlan an Arab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, but nothing is certain about his origin, ethnicity, education or the dates of his birth and death. From his own writing it appears that he was a faqih, an expert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>, and that he had served for some time at the court of the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir before his mission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 921 Ibn Fadlan was sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> as secretary to an ambassador of al-Muqtadir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alm-s">Almış</a>, the iltäbär or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> of Volga Bulgaria. The party, led by the eunuch Susan al-Rassi, left on 21 June 921. Its purpose was to explain Islamic law to the recently converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and to answer the Bulgar king's request for help against the Khazars. Ibn Fadlan served as religious adviser and counsellor on doctrine and law.</p><p>The party travelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, then turned north from what is now north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, it crossed the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>, covering some 4,000 kilometres in all. It reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> capital on 12 May 922. Ibn Fadlan read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s letter to the Bulgar ruler and presented the gifts, but was criticised for not bringing the money promised to build a fortress against the enemies of the Bulgars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His risāla, an account of the journey, long survived only in quotations in Yaqut's geographical dictionary, published in 1823 by Christian Martin Frähn. In 1923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeki-velidi-togan">Zeki Velidi Togan</a> found a fuller 13th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, which breaks off while describing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. Neither source seems to hold the complete report.</p><p>Ibn Fadlan judged the peoples he met largely by their practice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and he was angered by what he took to be unsound practice among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. His longest passage concerns the Rūs, generally taken to be Volga Vikings trading by the river. He describes their appearance, their weapons and the ship funeral of a chieftain, which involved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, and calls their hygiene shameless. He also gives early first-hand descriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, who lived in felt tents, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>. His text later inspired Michael Crichton's novel Eaters of the Dead.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad ibn Fadlan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their ruler bore the title yiltawar and the Khazars took tribute from his people, in furs paid per household according to <b>Ibn Fadlan</b>, and held one of his sons as a hostage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…as history in Latin, including the story of Amleth, the original of Shakespeare’s Hamlet; the German chronicler Adam of Bremen described the temple at Uppsala around 1075; and the Arab envoy <b>Ibn Fadlan</b> described the ship funeral of a chieftain of the Rus on the Volga in 922.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Arab traveller <b>Ibn Fadlan</b>, who met Rus’ merchants on the Volga in 922, described a chieftain burned in his ship after a slave girl had been killed to go with him.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The traveller <b>Ibn Fadlan</b>, who crossed their lands in 922, described their customs, military leaders and lack of Islam.</blockquote><a href="/state/oghuz-yabgu-state">Oghuz Yabgu State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=921" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad ibn Fadlan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=921">What was here in 921 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
